# sem-profile — UX design sheet prompt
# Display modes: the labeled profile chip; the id-fallback chip (no label);
# the implies closure shown through gating (operator sees reader content);
# class-form parity. One example page per mode on one sheet.
# Reference attachments are the judged renders from the snap harness
# (see reports/tag-sem-profile.md). Never run this generation from the
# tag lane — it is handed to the image model as written.

schema: "0.4"
id: sem-profile-ux-sheet
type: image
quality: high

prompt:
  text: |
    A precise UX design sheet for the SemText vocabulary element
    `sem-profile` (one named reader profile inside a declaration block),
    rendered as FOUR example pages in a 2x2 grid on a dark minimal-tech
    canvas (background #0f172a, card surface #1e293b, hairline borders
    #334155, violet accent, blue note markers).

    PAGE 1 — THE LABELED CHIP: one quiet horizontal metadata row inside a
    short document excerpt: a small-caps muted label "AUDIENCES ·"
    followed by two fully-rounded pill chips reading "Reader" and
    "Operator" — 1px hairline border, surface background, small legible
    text. The chip carries the profile's human label. This is metadata
    like a byline: no button affordance, no hover ring, no checkmark.

    PAGE 2 — THE ID-FALLBACK CHIP: the same strip spelling where one
    profile declares no label: its chip shows the bare id "operator" in
    lowercase beside a labeled "Reader" chip. The two chips must look
    identical in style — only the text differs; the id fallback is a
    first-class rendering, not a degraded or placeholder state.

    PAGE 3 — THE IMPLIES CLOSURE, SHOWN THROUGH GATING: the strip with
    "Reader" and "Operator" chips on top; beneath it ONE callout note
    (left accent border, rounded card) marked as reader content, fully
    legible; above the page a small muted monospace URL caption
    "…#sem-audience=operator". An annotation arrow or caption notes the
    operator chip satisfies reader content — the closure is the point.
    The page demonstrates the ACTIVE state: as operator, the reader note
    is present.

    PAGE 4 — CLASS FORM, IDENTICAL RENDER: the same strip spelled as
    `div.sem-audiences > div.sem-profile[id][data-label][data-implies]`,
    visually indistinguishable from page 1: same "AUDIENCES ·" label,
    same chips, same quiet metadata row.

    Typography: clean humanist sans for prose, small-caps muted strip
    label, monospace only for the URL caption and the attribute names.
    Flat, engineered, restrained — a documentation specimen, not an
    illustration.
  negative: |
    no chips rendered as buttons with shadows, hover, pressed or selected
    states; no toggle switches, dropdowns, or profile-picker UI anywhere —
    the strip is inert metadata and the profile is chosen by URL, not by
    clicking a chip; no occluded, blurred, black-barred or covered text —
    hidden content is absent from flow, never redacted in place; no
    "click to reveal" controls; no light-mode canvas; no decorative
    photos, icons, gradient glows, watermarks or browser chrome; no
    arrows drawn from the chips that imply the chips are clickable.
  style: flat dark technical documentation specimen, minimal-tech design system

attachments:
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-profile/sem-profile-primary-render-1280.png
    role: reference
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-profile/sem-profile-id-chip-render-1280.png
    role: reference
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-profile/sem-profile-implies-closure-render-1280.png
    role: reference
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-profile/sem-profile-class-form-split-1280.png
    role: reference
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-profile/sem-profile-implies-closure-render-390.png
    role: reference

output:
  formats:
    - format: png
      filename: sem-profile-ux-sheet.png
  dimensions:
    width: 2048
    height: 1152
    aspect_ratio: "16:9"

eval:
  pass_threshold: 0.7
  criteria:
    chip_metadata_not_control:
      weight: 0.25
      description: >
        Every profile chip reads as quiet metadata (hairline pill, small
        label text); nothing looks pressable, selected, or interactive.
      fail_signals:
        - chips with shadows, hover rings, or a checked/pressed state
        - a picker, toggle, or dropdown drawn beside the strip
    id_fallback_not_degraded:
      weight: 0.25
      description: >
        Page 2's id chip ("operator") is styled exactly like the labeled
        chip; only the text casing/content differs.
      fail_signals:
        - the id chip dimmed, dashed, greyed, or drawn as a placeholder
        - the two chips differing in shape or weight
    closure_shown_through_gating:
      weight: 0.2
      description: >
        Page 3 shows the operator-active state: the reader-content note
        present and legible, with the hash-parameter caption tying the
        state to navigation.
      fail_signals:
        - the reader note missing or occluded on the operator page
        - a visible picker that "chose" operator on the page itself
    form_parity:
      weight: 0.15
      description: >
        The class-form page is visually indistinguishable from page 1.
      fail_signals:
        - chips styled differently on the class-form page
    absence_not_occlusion:
      weight: 0.15
      description: >
        Wherever content is gated out, it is fully absent from flow — no
        reserved gap, outline, blur, or cover remains.
      fail_signals:
        - empty reserved boxes where hidden content would sit
        - blurred or blacked-out text blocks
  reject_if:
    - any chip looks clickable, selected, or hovered
    - any text is occluded, blurred, or covered by an opaque block
    - light-mode canvas
    - watermark or browser chrome present

tags: [semtext, sem-profile, ux-sheet, audience-profiles, reference]
product_targets: [semtext.dev/reference]
